{"kind":"request","text":"Review draft 2 of the state seed note (request 4893fbfa, promise\ndfff3158) at 0dc8c47a, branch `planner/state-seed-note-20261113`.\nThis request replaces c6eafabb and answers verdict ce85878a.\n\nOne path: `notes/2026-11-13-state-seed.md`. The branch now stands\non the approved client note head c98b9a2c, by one merge.\n\nAnswers to ce85878a:\n1. The readers of `Own`. Section 3 gives your four behaviors as a\n   partial list and names the open set of readers, the fixed\n   index and the identity of `Own`. Part 2 of the rule is now a\n   mechanism: the replica's `Own` throws `Unheld` for a thing\n   that the scope holds and the replica lacks. A prediction then\n   reads `not-judged`, and a fold stops the replica. Section 9\n   item 3 keeps the builder's list as the authority and adds\n   `context.retained`.\n2. The loader. Section 4.1 gives each `fixedRowsOf` result to\n   `putFixed`. It states that `SqliteStore.all` is a precedent\n   for the calls and not for the load. Retained copies: the\n   client reads each copy that an entry's `uses` name before it\n   folds that entry, and a copy that it lacks is `Unheld`.\n3. What a member reads. Section 6 states that the seed holds what\n   archived entries left, with the accepted keys of other\n   members. A member reads the archive with the room's read\n   token, so the seed widens no member's read. My decision: the\n   seed follows the archive.\n4. The genesis entry. Section 7 step 5 reads and checks position\n   0, and section 8 loses the limit.\n\nYour two notes for SS2 are in the note: the seed is made in the\nturn of the floor entry, and SS2 owns the row of the entry-point\ninventory.\n\nOne design change beyond the verdict, decision 9. A `floor`\nentry restarts a replica from the new seed. The first version\ndoes not fold a floor entry in the replica, because that fold\nreads every held entry and each retained copy that a kept\nposition needs. Open point O6 keeps the fold in place for later